  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088",
    "id": "q-80303",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-80303",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/0a7b19b50822235a2dcbfcdf74984735a1e26b2b775180b24bdc2bcbf24c588b.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n\nIII. $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only",
              "option": "0",
